WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:07.090
SPEAKER: Security
Health Analytics

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.880
is a native scanner in
Security Command Center.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.370
It assesses the overall
security state and activity

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.370
of your virtual machines,
containers, network, storage,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:18.850
and identity and access
management policies.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.100
The built-in capabilities
in the standard edition

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.650
includes scans for high
severity misconfigurations

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.780
such as open storage buckets.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.330
Using the premium edition of
Security Health Analytics,

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.060
you gain access to scans for
many more potential security

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:36.050
issues such as API keys
that need to be rotated.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.020
In addition, you get compliance
monitoring and security posture

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.060
management against
standards and frameworks

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:49.280
such as PCI DSS, CIS GCP 1.0,
[INAUDIBLE],, and ISO 27001.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.110
To enable Security
Health Analytics,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.300
you must have the Security
Center Admin Editor

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.900
role in Cloud IAM.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.480
When you're finished
adding roles, click Save.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.270
Then use the navigation window
to head to Security Command

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:03.620
Center.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.100
When you arrive, click
on Add Security Sources.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.950
Use the toggle to enable
Security Health Analytics

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.810
if it is not already.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:15.600
Then scroll down and click Save.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.300
Security Health analytics
scans can take up to 12 hours

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.130
to complete on the first run.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.500
After this initial run, it
will scan your resources

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.210
twice daily.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.880
The Vulnerabilities Dashboard
in Security Command Center

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.000
displays the Security
Health Analytics findings.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.430
If we sort the findings to
show account of active findings

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.550
with high severity, you can
see categories such as firewall

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.480
rules that allow public access.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.270
Scrolling a bit further, we
can see additional categories

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.220
for things like
Cloud storage buckets

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.040
that are publicly accessible,
instances configured

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.190
with public IP addresses,
instances with SSL

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:53.630
not being enforced, or resources
where the web UI isn't enabled.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.400
For a full list of
vulnerabilities,

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.050
follow the link in
our documentation

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.180
in the description below.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.030
Drilling in, you can filter
and view vulnerability findings

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.090
in many different
ways, like filtering

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.740
to one or more projects
or by characteristics

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.470
such as category, severity,
or applicable benchmarks.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.540
Security Health Analytics
can automatically

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.210
detect common vulnerabilities
and misconfigurations

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.640
across a multitude
of GCP products,

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.367
and more categories are
continually being added.

00:02:21.367 --> 00:02:23.200
After you filter by the
vulnerabilities that

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.120
are important to you,
you can click through

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.250
to view detailed information
about the findings.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.560
Let's use the Category
filter to take a look

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.100
at the open firewall findings.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.230
Drilling into a
specific finding,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.700
you can see a short
description of the issue,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.407
including the asset affected
and what it was detected by.

00:02:45.407 --> 00:02:47.490
Under Attributes, you can
see when the finding was

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.100
most recently seen and
when it was first found

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.660
and the source URI.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.120
The recommendation tells us
to complete two simple steps

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.683
to resolve this finding.

00:02:55.683 --> 00:02:58.350
You can easily head to the link,
which takes you to the resource

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.840
to fix the issue, in a new tab.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.020
All you need to do is follow the
recommendations and click Save.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.150
Here, I am editing
the firewall rules

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.830
to restrict the
range of source IPs

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.017
that are allowed connection
to a specific project's

00:03:10.017 --> 00:03:10.725
virtual machines.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.850
Congratulations.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.770
You've enabled Security
Health Analytics

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.650
and learned how to
respond to its findings.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

